{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\ntotal 780\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 claude claude   4096 Oct 14 14:47 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 claude claude   4096 Oct 14 10:11 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude    731 Oct 14 14:41 brief.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude   6459 Oct 14 14:47 implementation.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude 759709 Oct 14 14:48 session.log\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude   6396 Oct 14 14:36 tasks.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude   1224 Oct 14 14:48 tokens.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude    436 Oct 14 10:11 verdict.md\n# Brief: Swipe from top to reload\n\ntype: feature\nid: object\nbranch: feature/object_swipe-from-top-to-reload\ndate: 2026-10-14\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nOur mobile app has been released to f-droid.\nOne feedback: you cannot swipe down from the top to reload which is default behavior on most apps and necessary, e.g. if something didn't load properly or something was updated elsewhere.\nPlease implement swipe from the top down to reload and show the kind of animated indicator you would expect from apps.\n\n## Why\n\n<!-- Why does this need to exist? What problem does it solve for the user? -->\n\n## Out of scope\n\n<!-- What are we explicitly NOT doing in this job? -->\n\n## Notes\n\nYou are allowed to add dependencies if needed \nfeature/object_swipe-from-top-to-reload\n# Tasks: Swipe from top to reload\n\nid: object\nanalyst: @analyst\ndate: 2026-10-14\n\n## Context (what the analyst found)\n\n- Branch verified: worktree HEAD is `feature/object_swipe-from-top-to-reload`, matching `brief.md`.\n- This repo (`mobile`) is a Capacitor 7 Android shell. `frontend/` is **vendored**\n  from the canonical app repo (`../app`) by `scripts/vendor.sh` (`rsync --delete`),\n  with mobile-only overrides kept in `patch/`. Any edit made directly in\n  `frontend/src` will be **wiped on the next vendor/release run**, so\n  this job must not change `frontend/`.\n- Because of that, the plan is a **native Android** pull-to-refresh: wrap the\n  Capacitor WebView in AndroidX `SwipeRefreshLayout`, which gives the standard\n  Material circular spinner that Android users expect. Everything stays in\n  `android/`.\n- **Key pitfall:** the web app never scrolls the document itself. In\n  `frontend/src/routes/+layout.svelte` the root `.page-container` is\n  `overflow-hidden` and the content scrolls inside\n  `<div class=\"... overflow-auto content-container\">`, and there are other nested\n  scrollers too (modals, kanban, calendar views). So `WebView.getScrollY()` /\n  `canScrollVertically(-1)` will always say \"at top\", and a plain\n  `SwipeRefreshLayout` would start a refresh whenever the user scrolls a list\n  back up. The native side has to ask the page whether the element under the\n  finger is scrolled to the top (TASK-3).\n- Capacitor's `BridgeActivity` sets its own content layout\n  (`capacitor_bridge_layout_main`); `android/app/src/main/res/layout/activity_main.xml`\n  looks unused. So wrapping should probably happen in code in `MainActivity`\n  (re-parent the bridge WebView), not in XML. The developer should check this\n  against the installed `@capacitor/android` sources before starting TASK-2.\n\n## Open questions / assumptions (not blocking; flag if wrong)\n\n1. **New Gradle dependency.** `androidx.swiperefreshlayout:swiperefreshlayout`\n   is not on the classpath yet. It is a standard AndroidX library from Google\n   Maven, which F-Droid builds accept. Project rules say \"never install packages\n   without asking\". That rule is written for npm, but the developer/reviewer\n   should get explicit sign-off on this addition.\n2. **What \"reload\" means.** Assumption: a full `WebView.reload()` of the current\n   route. It is simple and also fixes \"didn't load properly\" cases. Side effect:\n   in-memory UI state is lost (e.g. an unsaved note in the editor or an open\n   modal). A softer \"re-fetch data only\" option would need changes in the\n   vendored frontend and is out of scope here.\n3. **Where the gesture is disabled.** Assumption: no refresh when the touch\n   starts inside a scrolled element, inside an open modal/dialog or bottom\n   sheet, or during a drag (kanban dnd, calendar entry drag). Exact rules are\n   settled in TASK-3.\n4. Spinner colours: assumption is to reuse the existing\n   `solyto_bg_light/dark` colours plus a contrasting accent. No new design input.\n\n## Task breakdown\n\nTASK-1: Add the AndroidX SwipeRefreshLayout dependency (version pinned in `variables.gradle`) and confirm the project still builds.\n  files: android/variables.gradle, android/app/build.gradle\n  depends: none (needs dependency sign-off, see open question 1)\n  risk: low — a single well-known AndroidX artifact, but it must resolve in the F-Droid build environment (`scripts/build-fdroid.sh`).\n\nTASK-2: In `MainActivity`, wrap the Capacitor bridge WebView in a `SwipeRefreshLayout` in code (keep its layout params and edge-to-edge inset handling), and on refresh call `webView.reload()` and stop the spinner when the page finishes loading.\n  files: android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/MainActivity.java\n  depends: TASK-1\n  risk: medium — re-parenting t\n\n... [4134 characters truncated] ...\n\nty` uses `capacitor_bridge_layout_main`, and\n`CapacitorWebView.edgeToEdgeHandler` insets the WebView by setting its own\nlayout margins. `SwipeRefreshLayout` ignores child margins, so the extra\nFrameLayout is needed to keep Capacitor's edge-to-edge handling working. The\nexisting margins are copied over and insets are re-requested. On refresh,\n`webView.reload()` is called. The spinner stops through a\n`Bridge.addWebViewListener` (`onPageLoaded` / `onReceivedError`), which leaves\nCapacitor's own WebViewClient untouched, plus a 15 s timeout as a fallback.\nThe share-intent `loadUrl` is unchanged.\n\nTASK-3: Two new classes in `app.solyto`:\n- `PullToRefreshPlugin`: a Capacitor plugin with no methods, registered in\n  `MainActivity.onCreate` before `super.onCreate`. I made it a plugin so that\n  `addJavascriptInterface` runs during bridge setup, before the first page\n  loads (interfaces added later only appear after the next reload). After each\n  page load it injects a script (guarded so it installs only once per page).\n  On `touchstart` the script reports \"blocked\" if any of these is true:\n  - more than one finger is down, or the page is pinch-zoomed\n  - the document or any ancestor of the target has `scrollTop > 0`\n  - the target is inside `dialog[open]`, `[role=dialog|alertdialog]`,\n    `[aria-modal=true]` or any `position: fixed` element (this covers\n    `PopupContentModal`, the mobile nav overlays and the FAB)\n  - an ancestor has a `touch-action` that excludes vertical panning (e.g. maps)\n\n  If a page handler calls `preventDefault()` on `touchmove`, as\n  svelte-dnd-action does once a drag starts, the script also reports blocked\n  mid-gesture. If a page has no script (still loading, or an error page), the\n  check falls back to `webView.canScrollVertically(-1)`.\n- `PullToRefreshLayout`: a `SwipeRefreshLayout` subclass that handles the\n  timing race. `ACTION_DOWN` is always let through, so the layout records the\n  start point. Move events then stay blocked until the page answers for that\n  touch, or until 300 ms pass, after which a pull is allowed.\n\nI checked the injected script with `node --check`, and ran it in headless\nChromium against a fixture page with a mocked bridge. Results: plain element at\ntop → allowed; inside a scrolled list → blocked; fixed modal → blocked;\n`touch-action:none` → blocked; `touch-action:pan-y` → allowed;\n`preventDefault` on touchmove → blocked mid-gesture; a second injection only\nre-reports ready.\n\nTASK-4: Spinner colours follow the system light/dark mode and are re-applied\nin `onConfigurationChanged` (the manifest handles `uiMode` itself). The circle\nbackground is `solyto_bg_light` (#FFFFFF) in light mode and the new\n`solyto_surface_dark` (#222222, the web app's `s-dark-2`) in dark mode. The\narrow cycles through the app's gradient colours, `solyto_lightblue` #14A5CD and\n`solyto_green` #61D96A (new entries in `colors.xml`). The spinner position is\ntied to the WebView's top: a layout listener calls `setProgressViewOffset`, so\non Android 15+ edge-to-edge the spinner rests 64 dp below the status bar\ninstead of under it. Below Android 15 the top is 0 and SwipeRefreshLayout's\ndefaults are kept.\n\nTASK-5: **Not done.** No device verification was possible: this environment\nhas no Java toolchain, Android SDK or emulator, so I could neither build the\nAPK nor run it. Only the parts that run in a browser were checked (TASK-3,\nabove). Checklist still to run on a device/emulator (Android 14 and 15+, light\nand dark):\n- [ ] project builds (`./gradlew assembleDebug`) and the dependency resolves\n      in the F-Droid build\n- [ ] pull at the top of a page shows the spinner and reloads\n- [ ] scrolling a long list back up does not reload\n- [ ] no reload inside a modal/bottom sheet, kanban drag or calendar drag\n- [ ] spinner appears below the status bar on Android 15+ and the WebView\n      still has correct edge-to-edge margins\n- [ ] share intent (\"Share → solyto\") still opens `/share`\n\nNo changelog line was added: `fastlane/.../changelogs/` only has `10012.txt`,\nso the release process doesn't seem to expect one per version.\n\n## Known issues / follow-ups\n\n- The code has not been compiled or tested on a device (see TASK-5). This must\n  be done before release.\n- A reload is a full `WebView.reload()`, so unsaved in-memory UI state (e.g. an\n  open editor or modal) is lost. A softer data-only refresh would need\n  frontend changes in the `app` repo.\n- Spinner colours follow the system dark mode, the same as the existing\n  system-bar code, not the web app's own theme setting (`html.dark`).\n- On Android 15+ the spinner starts just above the WebView top, i.e. behind\n  the transparent status bar, while it is being pulled in. This is\n  cosmetic; please check on a device.\n- `MainActivity` already called `handleShareIntent` twice on cold start\n  (`BridgeActivity.load()` calls `onNewIntent`, then `onCreate` calls it\n  again). This is unchanged and out of scope.\ncat: /workspace/.manigot/manigot.json: No such file or directory","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QmH4znwiSoYYCkGNYndB5x"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0df14cb-790b-4cc9-af4f-67f3c4d6310e","uuid":"97b1b9ee-b614-407a-93cf-26c7060a8f2e","timestamp":"2026-10-14T14:48:01.606Z","tool_use_result":"Error: Exit code 1\ntotal 780\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 claude claude   4096 Oct 14 14:47 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 claude claude   4096 Oct 14 10:11 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude    731 Oct 14 14:41 brief.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude   6459 Oct 14 14:47 implementation.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude 759709 Oct 14 14:48 session.log\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude   6396 Oct 14 14:36 tasks.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude   1224 Oct 14 14:48 tokens.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 claude claude    436 Oct 14 10:11 verdict.md\n# Brief: Swipe from top to reload\n\ntype: feature\nid: object\nbranch: feature/object_swipe-from-top-to-reload\ndate: 2026-10-14\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nOur mobile app has been released to f-droid.\nOne feedback: you cannot swipe down from the top to reload which is default behavior on most apps and necessary, e.g. if something didn't load properly or something was updated elsewhere.\nPlease implement swipe from the top down to reload and show the kind of animated indicator you would expect from apps.\n\n## Why\n\n<!-- Why does this need to exist? What problem does it solve for the user? -->\n\n## Out of scope\n\n<!-- What are we explicitly NOT doing in this job? -->\n\n## Notes\n\nYou are allowed to add dependencies if needed \nfeature/object_swipe-from-top-to-reload\n# Tasks: Swipe from top to reload\n\nid: object\nanalyst: @analyst\ndate: 2026-10-14\n\n## Context (what the analyst found)\n\n- Branch ve