{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016D3LRu5YeehYLu1QhsvSYd","type":"tool_result","content":"TASK-2: In `MainActivity`, wrap the Capacitor bridge WebView in a `SwipeRefreshLayout` in code (keep its layout params and edge-to-edge inset handling), and on refresh call `webView.reload()` and stop the spinner when the page finishes loading.\n  files: android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/MainActivity.java\n  depends: TASK-1\n  risk: medium — re-parenting the bridge WebView after `super.onCreate` could interfere with Capacitor's `adjustMarginsForEdgeToEdge: \"auto\"` insets and with the share-intent `loadUrl`. Finishing the refresh needs a page-load hook that doesn't replace Capacitor's own `WebViewClient` (e.g. a `BridgeWebViewClient` subclass / `WebViewListener`, or a timeout fallback).\n\nTASK-3: Gate the gesture with `setOnChildScrollUpCallback`, backed by a small script that the native side injects into the WebView (no `frontend/` edits). On touchstart, the script finds the nearest scrollable ancestor of the touch target and reports to native (JavascriptInterface or a cached flag) whether it can scroll up, or whether the touch is inside an open modal/dialog. Refresh is allowed only when nothing under the finger can scroll up.\n  files: android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/MainActivity.java (possibly a small new helper class in the same package)\n  depends: TASK-2\n  risk: high — this decides whether the feature feels right or keeps firing by accident. Without it, scrolling up in any list triggers a reload (see Context). It also has a timing race (touchstart in JS vs. native intercept), and the script has to be re-injected after each page load/reload.\n\nTASK-4: Style the indicator: spinner/background colours that follow light/dark mode (reusing the existing `solyto_bg_*` colours), and a start offset so it shows below the status bar under edge-to-edge on Android 15+.\n  files: android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/MainActivity.java, android/app/src/main/res/values/colors.xml\n  depends: TASK-2\n  risk: low — cosmetic only; the one thing to watch is the spinner hiding under the transparent status bar on API 35+.\n\nTASK-5: Manually verify on a device/emulator (Android <15 and 15+, light and dark): pull at top reloads; scrolling a long list back up does not; no trigger inside modals/bottom sheets, kanban drag or calendar drag; share-intent still works; record the results in implementation.md. Add a short changelog line for the next F-Droid version if the release process expects it.\n  files: docs/jobs/object_swipe-from-top-to-reload/implementation.md, fastlane/metadata/android/en-US/changelogs/<next versionCode>.txt (only if wanted)\n  depends: TASK-3, TASK-4\n  risk: low — verification only. The repo has no automated UI tests, so this is the only check of the gesture behaviour.\n\n## Out of scope (by this plan)\n\n- Any change in `frontend/` (vendored; it would be overwritten). A web-based or\n  PWA pull-to-refresh would belong in the `app` repo as a separate job.\n- Soft data re-fetch without a full reload.\n- Version bump / release (`scripts/release.sh`).\n----\n# Verdict: Swipe from top to reload\n\nid: object\nreviewer: @reviewer\ndate: 2026-10-14\n\n## Review\n\nBranch checked: `feature/object_swipe-from-top-to-reload` (matches brief).\nBase: `main` (`.manigot/manigot.json` is missing, so I used the default).\nI reviewed the whole `git diff main...HEAD`. Every source change is in `android/`;\n`frontend/` was not touched, as the plan required.\n\nI could not compile anything either: there is no JDK, Gradle, Android SDK or\n`node_modules/@capacitor` here. So the Java was checked by reading only, against\nthe Capacitor 7.6.8 sources the developer quoted in `session.log`\n(`CapacitorWebView.edgeToEdgeHandler`, `WebViewListener`, `BridgeActivity`).\n\nTASK-1: PARTIAL\nnotes: `android/variables.gradle` pins `androidxSwipeRefreshLayoutVersion = '1.1.0'`\nand `android/app/build.gradle` uses it. That part is correct, and the brief\nallows new dependencies. The task also says \"confirm the project still builds\",\nand that was not done: the dependency has never been resolved and the new code\nhas never been compiled.\n\nTASK-2: PASS\nnotes: `MainActivity.setupPullToRefresh()` re-parents the WebView as\nSwipeRefreshLayout → FrameLayout → WebView. The FrameLayout is needed:\nCapacitor's edge-to-edge listener writes `MarginLayoutParams` on the WebView\nitself, and `SwipeRefreshLayout` ignores child margins. The existing margins\nare copied into `FrameLayout.LayoutParams`, and insets are re-requested. The\nspinner stops through `Bridge.addWebViewListener` (`onPageLoaded` /\n`onReceivedError`), so Capacitor's own WebViewClient is left alone. There is\nalso a 15 s timeout as a fallback. The share-intent code is unchanged.\n\nTASK-3: PASS\nnotes: The plugin is registered before `super.onCreate`, so `load()` adds the\nJS interface before `loadWebView()`. The ready flag is reset in\n`onPageStarted`, and the script is re-injected in `onPageLoaded`.\n`PullToRefreshLayout` always lets `ACTION_DOWN` through so the start Y is\nrecorded, then blocks move events until the page answers or 300 ms pass. This\nfits SwipeRefreshLayout 1.1.0's intercept logic. The blocking rules are\nreasonable for this frontend. The root `.page-container` /\n`.content-container` are not `position: fixed`, so a normal pull still works.\nThe calendar views' `touch-action: pan-y` is allowed. The JS interface only\nexposes `ready()` and `report(boolean)`, so there is no meaningful attack\nsurface. Release builds use `minifyEnabled false`, so ProGuard won't strip it.\n\nTASK-4: PASS\nnotes: The light/dark colours are re-applied in `onConfigurationChanged`. The\nnew colours are added to `colors.xml`. `setProgressViewOffset` is tied to\n`webView.getTop()`, and it is never called while a refresh is running.\n\nTASK-5: FAIL\nnotes: Not done (the developer says so openly in `implementation.md`). No\nbuild, 